export const c12Phy5HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n  \n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📖 Quick Glossary &amp; Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Core Concepts &amp; Key Definitions &bull; Unit V: Electromagnetic Waves</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #00E5FF; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>1. Displacement Current (I<sub>d</sub>):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Current arising due to a time-varying electric field / electric flux across empty space or a dielectric: I<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub>/dt) = C (dV/dt) [Ampere A, Dimensions: [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup> A<sup>1</sup>]].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #00E5FF; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>2. Ampere-Maxwell Law:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Generalized law stating that line integral of magnetic field around any closed loop equals &mu;<sub>0</sub> times total current (conduction current I<sub>c</sub> + displacement current I<sub>d</sub>): &oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> [I<sub>c</sub> + &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub>/dt)].</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #00E5FF; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>3. Electromagnetic (EM) Wave:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Self-sustaining wave consisting of mutually perpendicular, time-varying sinusoidal electric (<b>E</b>) and magnetic (<b>B</b>) fields oscillating perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation with vacuum speed c = 1/&radic;(&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) &approx; 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #00E5FF; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>4. Transverse Nature of EM Waves:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Property that electric vector <b>E</b> and magnetic vector <b>B</b> oscillate in directions strictly perpendicular to each other and perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation <b>k̂</b> (i.e., <b>E</b> &sdot; <b>k̂</b> = 0 and <b>B</b> &sdot; <b>k̂</b> = 0).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #00E5FF; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>5. Radiation Pressure &amp; Momentum (P<sub>rad</sub>, p):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Pressure exerted by electromagnetic radiation on a surface: P<sub>rad</sub> = I/c (for total absorption) or P<sub>rad</sub> = 2I/c (for total reflection) [Pascal Pa], with linear momentum transferred p = U/c.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style='color: #00E5FF; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;'>6. Electromagnetic Spectrum:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Continuous, orderly arrangement of all electromagnetic waves classified according to their frequency or wavelength: Gamma rays &rarr; X-rays &rarr; Ultraviolet &rarr; Visible light &rarr; Infrared &rarr; Microwaves &rarr; Radio waves.</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00E5FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00E5FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">1. Basic Idea of Displacement Current &amp; Ampere-Maxwell Law</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Historical Background &amp; Inconsistency of Ampere's Circuital Law</h3>\n<p>In 1864, Scottish physicist <b>James Clerk Maxwell</b> identified a major mathematical and physical flaw in Ampere's Circuital Law. Ampere's original circuital law states that the line integral of magnetic field <b>B</b> around any closed closed loop C equals &mu;<sub>0</sub> times the total electric current I enclosed by that loop:<br><span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>&oint;<sub>C</sub> <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>enclosed</sub></b></span>.</p>\n<p>Maxwell considered the charging of a parallel plate capacitor through a battery. Consider a circular loop C outside the capacitor plates:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>• <b>Surface S<sub>1</sub> (Flat disc bounded by loop C):</b> Passing directly through the connecting wire, it intersects the conduction current I<sub>c</sub> flowing into the capacitor. Thus, according to Ampere's law: <b>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>c</sub> &ne; 0</b>.</li>\n    <li>• <b>Surface S<sub>2</sub> (Pot-shaped open bag bounded by same loop C):</b> This surface bulges out and passes through the empty gap between the capacitor plates without intersecting any connecting wire. Because no physical charge travels across the insulating vacuum/dielectric gap, I<sub>enclosed</sub> = 0, which gives: <b>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = 0</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n<p>This was a glaring physical contradiction! The value of the line integral around the exact same closed boundary loop C cannot simultaneously be non-zero and zero depending on the arbitrary geometric surface chosen.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 1: Displacement Current &amp; Circulating Magnetic Fields\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 5.1:</b> Charging Parallel Plate Capacitor &ndash; Conduction current (I<sub>c</sub>) in the connecting wire and Displacement current (I<sub>d</sub>) due to time-varying electric field (&part;E/&part;t) in the gap, both producing continuous circulating magnetic field loops (B).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #38BDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Displacement Current as an Invisible Baton in a Relay Race</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Imagine a 4&times;100m relay race where runners (conduction electrons) carry a physical baton along the copper wire. At the capacitor plates, the runner stops, but throws an invisible beam of energy (time-varying electric field lines) across the gap to the next runner. To an outside observer watching the race, the momentum (magnetic effect) continues seamlessly without interruption! Conduction current on the wires and displacement current in the gap are two stages of the exact same continuous relay.</div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 25px;\">(ii) Mathematical Formulation of Displacement Current</h3>\n<p>Maxwell resolved this paradox by asserting that between the capacitor plates, as charge builds up, the electric field <b>E</b> increases with time. This changing electric field creates an electric flux &Phi;<sub>E</sub> that changes with time, generating a magnetic field in the gap just like a conventional electric current!</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Equivalence of Conduction Current and Displacement Current (I_c = I_d)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Electric Field between Plates):</b> For parallel plates of area A and charge q(t), the uniform electric field between them is:<br>\n    E(t) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&sigma;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q(t)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A</span></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Electric Flux &Phi;<sub>E</sub>):</b><br>\n    &Phi;<sub>E</sub>(t) = E(t) &times; A = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q(t)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A</span></span>] A = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q(t)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Time Rate of Change of Flux):</b> Differentiating with respect to time t:<br>\n    <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>E</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dq</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span> I<sub>c</sub> (where I<sub>c</sub> = dq/dt is the conduction current in connecting wires).<br>\n    <b>Step 4 (Defining Displacement Current I<sub>d</sub>):</b><br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>I<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>E</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dq</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = I<sub>c</sub></b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Alternative Expression for a Capacitor C:</b> Since q = C V(t) &rArr; <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>I<sub>d</sub> = C <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dV</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b></span>.\n  </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Displacement Current (I<sub>d</sub>)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Current produced in a region of space where the electric field and electric flux are changing with time: <b>I<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>E</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b>. It produces the exact same magnetic field as an equal conduction current.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Ampere (A)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup> A<sup>1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 25px;\">(iii) Ampere-Maxwell Modified Law &amp; Continuity of Total Current</h3>\n<p>Maxwell modified Ampere's circuital law by including both conduction current (I<sub>c</sub>) and displacement current (I<sub>d</sub>):</p>\n<div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 4px solid #76FF03; padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 6px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 17px;\"><b>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (I<sub>c</sub> + I<sub>d</sub>) = &mu;<sub>0</sub> [ I<sub>c</sub> + &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>E</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> ]</b></span>\n  </div>\n<p><b>Continuity of Total Current:</b> In the external connecting wires, the electric field is steady/negligible so I<sub>d</sub> = 0 and total current is I<sub>total</sub> = I<sub>c</sub>. In the vacuum gap between capacitor plates, no charges flow physically so I<sub>c</sub> = 0 and total current is I<sub>total</sub> = I<sub>d</sub>. Therefore, <b>total current I = I<sub>c</sub> + I<sub>d</sub> is strictly continuous</b> throughout the entire electrical circuit!</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 15.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 10px;\">⚡ Key Differences: Conduction Current (I<sub>c</sub>) vs Displacement Current (I<sub>d</sub>)</b>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(270px, 1fr)); gap: 10px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3.5px solid #38BDF8; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">Conduction Current (I<sub>c</sub>):</b>\n        <ul style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 16px; padding: 0; line-height: 1.6; color: #FFFFFF;\">\n          <li><b>Origin:</b> Physical flow of free conduction electrons.</li>\n          <li><b>Medium:</b> Requires a physical conductor (copper wires).</li>\n          <li><b>Formula:</b> I<sub>c</sub> = dq/dt = n A e v<sub>d</sub> = V/R.</li>\n          <li><b>Heating:</b> Generates Joule heating (I<sub>c</sub><sup>2</sup> R).</li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3.5px solid #76FF03; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #76FF03;\">Displacement Current (I<sub>d</sub>):</b>\n        <ul style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 16px; padding: 0; line-height: 1.6; color: #FFFFFF;\">\n          <li><b>Origin:</b> Time rate of change of electric flux (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub>/dt).</li>\n          <li><b>Medium:</b> Exists across insulators and <b>pure vacuum</b>.</li>\n          <li><b>Formula:</b> I<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub>/dt) = C (dV/dt).</li>\n          <li><b>Heating:</b> Produces <b>zero Joule heat</b> in vacuum.</li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.1); border: 1.2px solid rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">⚠️ BOARD EXAM TRAP &amp; CONCEPTUAL PITFALL: Does Displacement Current Flow When a Capacitor is Fully Charged?</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FCA5A5; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">When a capacitor is fully charged with a DC voltage source, the voltage becomes constant (dV/dt = 0) and the electric flux becomes static (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub>/dt = 0). Therefore, <b>displacement current I<sub>d</sub> = 0</b> in a fully charged DC capacitor! Displacement current exists ONLY during charging, discharging, or with alternating AC sources.</div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00E5FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00E5FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">2. Maxwell's Equations &amp; Origin of Electromagnetic Waves</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Maxwell's Four Fundamental Equations of Electromagnetism</h3>\n<p>Maxwell unified electricity, magnetism, and optics into a single master theory described by 4 fundamental equations:</p>\n  <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(270px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-left: 4px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 14.5px;\">1. Gauss's Law in Electrostatics</b>\n      <div style=\"font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03; margin: 4px 0 6px 0; font-size: 15px;\">&oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = q<sub>enclosed</sub> / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub></div>\n      <span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5;\">Electric field lines originate on positive charges and terminate on negative charges. Isolated electric charges exist.</span>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-left: 4px solid #38BDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 14.5px;\">2. Gauss's Law in Magnetism</b>\n      <div style=\"font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03; margin: 4px 0 6px 0; font-size: 15px;\">&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = 0</div>\n      <span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5;\">Magnetic field lines form continuous closed loops without start or end. <b>Isolated magnetic monopoles do not exist</b>.</span>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-left: 4px solid #A78BFA; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #A78BFA; font-size: 14.5px;\">3. Faraday-Lenz Law of Induction</b>\n      <div style=\"font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03; margin: 4px 0 6px 0; font-size: 15px;\">&oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = - d&Phi;<sub>B</sub> / dt</div>\n      <span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5;\">A time-varying magnetic field produces an induced, non-conservative electric field in space.</span>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-left: 4px solid #F472B6; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #F472B6; font-size: 14.5px;\">4. Ampere-Maxwell Law</b>\n      <div style=\"font-family: monospace; color: #76FF03; margin: 4px 0 6px 0; font-size: 14px;\">&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> [I<sub>c</sub> + &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub>/dt)]</div>\n      <span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5;\">Magnetic fields are generated both by conduction current (I<sub>c</sub>) and by <b>time-varying electric fields (I<sub>d</sub>)</b>.</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 25px;\">(ii) Source &amp; Mechanism of Electromagnetic Wave Generation</h3>\n<p>How are electromagnetic waves generated? Consider different states of motion of an electric charge q:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>1. <b>Charge at Rest (v = 0, a = 0):</b> Produces only a static electrostatic field <b>E</b> (No magnetic field, no radiation).</li>\n    <li>2. <b>Charge Moving with Uniform Velocity (v = const, a = 0):</b> Produces a steady electric current and a static magnetic field <b>B</b>. The magnetic field does not change with time, so <b>no EM waves are radiated</b>.</li>\n    <li>3. <b>Accelerating / Oscillating Electric Charge (a &ne; 0):</b> When a charge accelerates (such as an electron oscillating in an LC circuit or dipole antenna with frequency &nu;), it produces a time-varying electric field <b>E(t)</b>. By Ampere-Maxwell law, this changing electric field creates a time-varying magnetic field <b>B(t)</b>. By Faraday's law, this changing magnetic field in turn creates a changing electric field! This mutual regeneration propagates endlessly outward through space as an <b>Electromagnetic Wave</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Speed of Electromagnetic Waves in Vacuum and Dielectric Media</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Maxwell's Wave Equations in Vacuum with zero charge and current &rho; = 0, J = 0):</b><br>\n    <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;<sup>2</sup>E</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;x<sup>2</sup></span></span> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;<sup>2</sup>E</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;t<sup>2</sup></span></span>, &nbsp;&nbsp; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;<sup>2</sup>B</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;x<sup>2</sup></span></span> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;<sup>2</sup>B</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;t<sup>2</sup></span></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Comparison with Classical Wave Equation &part;<sup>2</sup>y/&part;x<sup>2</sup> = (1/v<sup>2</sup>) &part;<sup>2</sup>y/&part;t<sup>2</sup>):</b><br>\n    <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c<sup>2</sup></span></span> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)</span></span></b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Numerical Calculation):</b> Substituting &mu;<sub>0</sub> = 4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> N A<sup>-2</sup> and &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = 8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> C<sup>2</sup> N<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-2</sup>:<br>\n    c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;[(4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>)(8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>)]</span></span> = <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>2.9979 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s &approx; 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s</b></span>.<br>\n    <i>Historic Triumph:</i> This calculated velocity matched the experimentally measured speed of light, proving light is an electromagnetic wave!<br>\n    <b>Speed in Material Dielectric Medium (Refractive Index n):</b><br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>v = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(&mu; &epsilon;)</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(&mu;<sub>r</sub> &epsilon;<sub>r</sub>)</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n</span></span></b></span> (where n = &radic;(&mu;<sub>r</sub> &epsilon;<sub>r</sub>) &approx; &radic;&epsilon;<sub>r</sub> for non-magnetic media).\n  </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00E5FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00E5FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">3. Characteristics &amp; Transverse Nature of Electromagnetic Waves</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Mathematical Description of a Plane Sinusoidal EM Wave</h3>\n<p>For a plane electromagnetic wave traveling along the <b>+x axis</b>:</p>\n<ul style=\"font-family: monospace; font-size: 15px; color: #76FF03; line-height: 1.8;\">\n    <li>• Electric field vector (oscillating along z-axis): <b>E</b><sub>z</sub>(x, t) = E<sub>0</sub> sin(k x - &omega; t) <b>k̂</b></li>\n    <li>• Magnetic field vector (oscillating along y-axis): <b>B</b><sub>y</sub>(x, t) = B<sub>0</sub> sin(k x - &omega; t) <b>ĵ</b></li>\n  </ul>\n<p>where E<sub>0</sub>, B<sub>0</sub> are amplitudes, k = 2&pi;/&lambda; is propagation wavevector, and &omega; = 2&pi;&nu; is angular frequency. Wave speed is <b>c = &omega;/k = &nu; &lambda;</b>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 2: Electromagnetic Waves (3D Representation)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 5.2:</b> 3D Spatial Representation of a Plane Electromagnetic Wave propagating along the x-axis with oscillating Electric Field (E-field along z-axis, blue) and Magnetic Field (B-field along y-axis, red).</p>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 25px;\">(ii) Proof of Transverse Nature (Qualitative Idea)</h3>\n<p>In a mechanical longitudinal wave (like sound), particles oscillate parallel to the direction of propagation. In an electromagnetic wave:</p>\n<ul>\n    <li>1. Electric field <b>E</b> and magnetic field <b>B</b> are strictly perpendicular to each other: <b><b>E</b> &sdot; <b>B</b> = 0</b>.</li>\n    <li>2. Both <b>E</b> and <b>B</b> are perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation vector <b>k̂</b>: <b><b>E</b> &sdot; <b>k̂</b> = 0</b> and <b><b>B</b> &sdot; <b>k̂</b> = 0</b>.</li>\n    <li>3. The direction of wave propagation is given by the cross product of unit vectors: <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>k̂ = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\"><b>E</b> &times; <b>B</b></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|<b>E</b> &times; <b>B</b>|</span></span></b></span> (Right-Hand Rule: rotate fingers from <b>E</b> to <b>B</b>, thumb points in propagation direction).</li>\n    <li>4. <b>Experimental Confirmation (Polarization):</b> Because EM waves can be polarized (demonstrated with polaroid filters and calcite crystals), EM waves are definitively proven to be <b>transverse waves</b> (longitudinal waves cannot exhibit polarization!).</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 420px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 3: Transverse Nature of Electromagnetic Waves\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 5.3:</b> Mutual Perpendicularity &ndash; At any point of observation, the electric field vector (E), magnetic field vector (B), and wave propagation vector (c) are all strictly mutually perpendicular (E &perp; B &perp; c).</p>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 25px;\">(iii) 10 Key Fundamental Characteristics of Electromagnetic Waves</h3>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n      <li>1. <b>Self-Sustaining &amp; No Medium Needed:</b> EM waves do not require any material medium for propagation and travel freely through vacuum.</li>\n      <li>2. <b>Universal Vacuum Speed:</b> All EM waves (from radio waves to gamma rays) travel in vacuum with the identical constant speed: <b>c = 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s</b>.</li>\n      <li>3. <b>Ratio of Amplitudes:</b> The ratio of electric field amplitude to magnetic field amplitude at every instant equals the speed of light: <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">E<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>0</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">E</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B</span></span> = c</b></span>.</li>\n      <li>4. <b>Equal Energy Partition:</b> The total energy of an EM wave is split <b>exactly 50% in the electric field and 50% in the magnetic field</b>:<br>\n      Average Electric Energy Density: <b>u<sub>E</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span></span> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></b><br>\n      Average Magnetic Energy Density: <b>u<sub>B</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span> = u<sub>E</sub></b><br>\n      Total Average Energy Density: <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>u<sub>avg</sub> = u<sub>E</sub> + u<sub>B</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup></b></span>.</li>\n      <li>5. <b>Intensity of Radiation (I):</b> Average energy crossing per unit area per unit second perpendicular to propagation direction:<br>\n      <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>I = u<sub>avg</sub> &times; c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> c = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup> c</b></span> [W/m<sup>2</sup>].</li>\n      <li>6. <b>Poynting Vector (<b>S</b>):</b> Vector representing instantaneous rate of energy flow per unit area:<br>\n      <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b><b>S</b> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span> (<b>E</b> &times; <b>B</b>)</b></span> [W/m<sup>2</sup>].</li>\n      <li>7. <b>Linear Momentum (p):</b> An EM wave carries momentum. When energy U is delivered to a surface:<br>\n      • Completely Absorbing Surface: <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>p = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">U</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span></b></span><br>\n      • Completely Reflecting Surface: <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>p = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 U</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span></b></span> (reverses momentum upon reflection).</li>\n      <li>8. <b>Radiation Pressure (P<sub>rad</sub>):</b> Force per unit area exerted by EM wave on a surface:<br>\n      • Absorbing surface: <b>P<sub>rad</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span></b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Reflecting surface: <b>P<sub>rad</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span></b> [N/m<sup>2</sup> or Pa].</li>\n      <li>9. <b>Superposition &amp; Wave Optics:</b> EM waves obey the superposition principle, exhibiting interference, diffraction, reflection, and refraction.</li>\n      <li>10. <b>Optical Vector:</b> Because the electric field vector <b>E</b> produces dominant physiological and chemical effects on the retina and photographic plates (F<sub>e</sub>/F<sub>m</sub> = E/(v B) &approx; c/v &gt;&gt; 1), <b>E is called the optical vector</b>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00E5FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00E5FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">4. The Electromagnetic Spectrum: Wavelength Bands, Production &amp; Practical Applications</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Master Overview &amp; Order of Spectrum Bands</h3>\n<p>The electromagnetic spectrum extends seamlessly from low-frequency radio waves to ultra-high-frequency gamma rays. There are no sharp boundaries; adjacent regions overlap based on production mechanism:</p>\n<p class=\"text-center\" style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.4); padding: 12px; border-radius: 8px; color: #00E5FF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n    Increasing Frequency (&nu;) &amp; Photon Energy (E = h&nu;) &rarr;<br>\n    <b>Radio Waves &rarr; Microwaves &rarr; Infrared &rarr; Visible Light &rarr; Ultraviolet &rarr; X-Rays &rarr; Gamma Rays</b><br>\n    &larr; Increasing Wavelength (&lambda; = c/&nu;)\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.5px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35); text-align: center;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); margin: 0 auto; max-width: 600px;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Diagram 4: Electromagnetic Spectrum Overview\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n    </div>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0; text-align: center !important;\"><b>Fig 5.4:</b> Complete Electromagnetic Spectrum Overview &ndash; Showing the continuous frequency and wavelength progression across Radio waves, Microwaves, Infrared, Visible light (400 nm &ndash; 700 nm), Ultraviolet, X-Rays, and Gamma Rays.</p>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 25px;\">(ii) Comprehensive Breakdown &amp; Complete Details for Each of the 7 Spectrum Bands:</h3>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.4px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">1. Radio Waves</span>\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.15); color: #00E5FF; padding: 3px 10px; border-radius: 20px; font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600;\">&lambda;: &gt; 0.1 m (&gt; 10 cm to several kilometers) &nbsp;|&nbsp; &nu;: 500 kHz to 1000 MHz (Up to 3 GHz)</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- SPECTRUM VISUAL + DETAILS GRID -->\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); max-width: 220px; margin: 0 auto;\">\n        <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Radio Waves\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 14px;\">\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">🏭 Production Source:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Rapid accelerated oscillation of conduction electrons in transmitting aerial antennas and tuned LC tank circuits.</span>\n        </div>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">📡 Detection Method:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Receiver antenna aerials coupled with tuned LC resonant circuits and radio frequency amplifiers.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #76FF03;\">🚀 Key Practical Applications &amp; Board Focus:</b><br><span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; line-height: 1.6;\">• <b>AM Broadcasting:</b> Standard Amplitude Modulation band (530 kHz to 1710 kHz).<br>• <b>Shortwave Radio:</b> High Frequency HF band (3 MHz to 30 MHz) reflected by atmospheric ionosphere.<br>• <b>Television &amp; FM Radio:</b> VHF (54 MHz to 216 MHz) &amp; UHF (470 MHz to 890 MHz) broadcasting.<br>• <b>Cellular Telephony:</b> Mobile communication links (800 MHz to 2.5 GHz).<br>• <b>Key Fact:</b> Longest wavelengths in the electromagnetic spectrum.</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.4px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">2. Microwaves</span>\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.15); color: #00E5FF; padding: 3px 10px; border-radius: 20px; font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600;\">&lambda;: 0.1 m to 1 mm (10<sup>-1</sup> m to 10<sup>-3</sup> m) &nbsp;|&nbsp; &nu;: 1 GHz to 300 GHz (10<sup>9</sup> Hz to 10<sup>11</sup> Hz)</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- SPECTRUM VISUAL + DETAILS GRID -->\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); max-width: 220px; margin: 0 auto;\">\n        <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Microwaves\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 14px;\">\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">🏭 Production Source:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Specialized vacuum tube oscillators including <b>Klystron tubes</b>, <b>Magnetron valves</b>, and solid-state <b>Gunn diodes</b>.</span>\n        </div>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">📡 Detection Method:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Point-contact crystal semiconductor diodes, horn antennae, and parabolic dish receivers.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #76FF03;\">🚀 Key Practical Applications &amp; Board Focus:</b><br><span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; line-height: 1.6;\">• <b>Microwave Ovens (Cooking):</b> Resonant heating at 2.45 GHz matching rotational frequency of water (H<sub>2</sub>O) molecules.<br>• <b>RADAR Aircraft Navigation:</b> Short wavelength allows high directional beam pointing for air traffic control, speed detection guns, and defense tracking.<br>• <b>Satellite &amp; Wireless Links:</b> Direct line-of-sight satellite links and Wi-Fi networks.<br>• <b>Key Fact:</b> Higher frequency and shorter wavelengths than radio waves.</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.4px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">3. Infrared Waves (Heat Waves)</span>\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.15); color: #00E5FF; padding: 3px 10px; border-radius: 20px; font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600;\">&lambda;: 1 mm to 700 nm (10<sup>-3</sup> m to 7 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> m) &nbsp;|&nbsp; &nu;: 3 &times; 10<sup>11</sup> Hz to 4 &times; 10<sup>14</sup> Hz</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- SPECTRUM VISUAL + DETAILS GRID -->\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); max-width: 220px; margin: 0 auto;\">\n        <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Infrared Waves (Heat Waves)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 14px;\">\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">🏭 Production Source:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Thermal vibrations and rotations of atoms and molecules in hot and glowing bodies (furnaces, incandescent filaments, Earth's crust).</span>\n        </div>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">📡 Detection Method:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Thermopiles, Bolometers, Pyroelectric sensors, and special infrared photographic emulsions.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #76FF03;\">🚀 Key Practical Applications &amp; Board Focus:</b><br><span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; line-height: 1.6;\">• <b>Household Remote Controls:</b> Infrared LEDs in TV, AC, and home appliance remote controllers.<br>• <b>Night-Vision &amp; Thermal Imaging:</b> Military night-vision binoculars, thermal imaging scopes, and security cameras.<br>• <b>Greenhouse Heat Warming:</b> Earth absorbs visible solar light and re-emits IR radiation trapped by greenhouse gases.<br>• <b>Physical Therapy:</b> Heat therapy lamps for muscular stiffness.<br>• <b>Key Fact:</b> Readily absorbed by matter and experienced directly as heat.</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.4px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">4. Visible Light</span>\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.15); color: #00E5FF; padding: 3px 10px; border-radius: 20px; font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600;\">&lambda;: 700 nm to 400 nm (7 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> m to 4 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> m / 4000 &Aring; to 7000 &Aring;) &nbsp;|&nbsp; &nu;: 4 &times; 10<sup>14</sup> Hz to 7.5 &times; 10<sup>14</sup> Hz</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- SPECTRUM VISUAL + DETAILS GRID -->\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); max-width: 220px; margin: 0 auto;\">\n        <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Visible Light\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 14px;\">\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">🏭 Production Source:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Atomic electron de-excitations when outer valence electrons transition from higher to lower quantum energy states in glowing solids and discharge tubes.</span>\n        </div>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">📡 Detection Method:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Human eye retina (rhodopsin pigment stimulation), Photoelectric cells, Photodiodes, Photographic film, CCD sensors.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #76FF03;\">🚀 Key Practical Applications &amp; Board Focus:</b><br><span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; line-height: 1.6;\">• <b>Human Sight &amp; Color Sensation:</b> Fundamental optical window (VIBGYOR) providing visual perception of the universe.<br>• <b>Photosynthesis:</b> Driving chemical synthesis of glucose from CO<sub>2</sub> and water in green plants.<br>• <b>Optical Illumination &amp; Microscopy:</b> General lighting, optical microscopes, and astronomical telescopes.<br>• <b>Key Fact:</b> Narrowest band in the spectrum (400 nm &ndash; 700 nm).</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.4px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">5. Ultraviolet (UV) Radiation</span>\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.15); color: #00E5FF; padding: 3px 10px; border-radius: 20px; font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600;\">&lambda;: 400 nm to 1 nm (4 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> m to 10<sup>-9</sup> m) &nbsp;|&nbsp; &nu;: 7.5 &times; 10<sup>14</sup> Hz to 10<sup>16</sup> Hz</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- SPECTRUM VISUAL + DETAILS GRID -->\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); max-width: 220px; margin: 0 auto;\">\n        <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Ultraviolet (UV) Radiation\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 14px;\">\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">🏭 Production Source:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Extremely high-temperature thermal sources (Sun, electric carbon arcs) and gas discharge lamps (mercury vapor lamps).</span>\n        </div>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">📡 Detection Method:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Photocells, Photographic plates, and Fluorescence induced on zinc sulfide / phosphor screens.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #76FF03;\">🚀 Key Practical Applications &amp; Board Focus:</b><br><span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; line-height: 1.6;\">• <b>Water Purification &amp; Sterilization:</b> Germicidal UV-C lamps (&lambda; &approx; 254 nm) killing bacteria and viruses in purifiers.<br>• <b>Skin Tanning &amp; Sun Damage:</b> Causes melanin production (tanning); overexposure causes sunburns and skin damage.<br>• <b>LASIK Eye Surgery &amp; Forensics:</b> Corneal ablation and detecting counterfeit currency via fluorescence.<br>• <b>Key Fact:</b> Highly absorbed and filtered by atmospheric stratospheric ozone (O<sub>3</sub>) layer.</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.4px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">6. X-Rays (Roentgen Rays)</span>\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.15); color: #00E5FF; padding: 3px 10px; border-radius: 20px; font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600;\">&lambda;: 1 nm to 10<sup>-3</sup> nm (10<sup>-9</sup> m to 10<sup>-12</sup> m / 10 &Aring; to 0.01 &Aring;) &nbsp;|&nbsp; &nu;: 10<sup>16</sup> Hz to 3 &times; 10<sup>19</sup> Hz</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- SPECTRUM VISUAL + DETAILS GRID -->\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); max-width: 220px; margin: 0 auto;\">\n        <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"X-Rays (Roentgen Rays)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 14px;\">\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">🏭 Production Source:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Sudden energetic deceleration of fast cathode electrons striking a heavy metal target of high melting point (Tungsten/Molybdenum) in a Coolidge X-ray tube.</span>\n        </div>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">📡 Detection Method:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Photographic film plates, Geiger-Muller counters, Ionization chambers, and digital flat-panel detectors.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #76FF03;\">🚀 Key Practical Applications &amp; Board Focus:</b><br><span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; line-height: 1.6;\">• <b>Medical Diagnostic Radiography:</b> Detecting bone fractures, dental cavities, and internal pulmonary lesions (CT scans).<br>• <b>Security Screening:</b> Non-destructive airport luggage scanners to identify weapons and contraband.<br>• <b>Crystallography:</b> Determining atomic crystal lattice structures via Bragg's diffraction.<br>• <b>Key Fact:</b> High energy ionizing radiation capable of penetrating soft flesh while stopped by dense bones.</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.4px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 18px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #00E5FF; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">7. Gamma (&gamma;) Rays</span>\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.15); color: #00E5FF; padding: 3px 10px; border-radius: 20px; font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600;\">&lambda;: &lt; 10<sup>-3</sup> nm (&lt; 10<sup>-12</sup> m to 10<sup>-15</sup> m) &nbsp;|&nbsp; &nu;: &gt; 3 &times; 10<sup>19</sup> Hz (Highest photon energy E = h&nu;)</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- SPECTRUM VISUAL + DETAILS GRID -->\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; align-items: center; margin-bottom: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; background: #FFFDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); max-width: 220px; margin: 0 auto;\">\n        <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Gamma (&gamma;) Rays\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border-radius: 6px; object-fit: contain;\" />\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; font-size: 14px;\">\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">🏭 Production Source:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Radioactive decay of unstable atomic nuclei, nuclear fission/fusion reactions, and nuclear isomer transitions (Cobalt-60, Caesium-137).</span>\n        </div>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n          <b style=\"color: #38BDF8;\">📡 Detection Method:</b><br><span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Scintillation counters, Geiger-Muller tubes, and semiconductor solid-state gamma detectors.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 12px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #76FF03;\">🚀 Key Practical Applications &amp; Board Focus:</b><br><span style=\"color: #E2E8F0; line-height: 1.6;\">• <b>Cancer Radiotherapy:</b> Focused gamma-knife beams destroying deep-seated malignant cancer tumors.<br>• <b>Cold Medical Sterilization:</b> Sterilization of pre-packaged surgical scalpels, syringes, and medical implants.<br>• <b>Industrial Radiography:</b> Inspecting internal microcracks in thick steel engine casings and weld seams.<br>• <b>Key Fact:</b> Highest frequency, highest photon energy, and most penetrating radiation in the entire electromagnetic spectrum.</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 25px;\">(iii) In-Depth Elementary Facts &amp; Board Focus Points</h3>\n<ul>\n    <li>• <b>Why Microwave Ovens Heat Water-Containing Foods Efficiently:</b> The microwave frequency (&approx; 2.45 GHz) matches the natural rotational resonant frequency of polar water (H<sub>2</sub>O) molecules. Water molecules rotate vigorously in resonance, converting absorbed microwave energy directly into thermal agitation heat.</li>\n    <li>• <b>The Vital Role of the Atmospheric Ozone Layer:</b> Stratospheric ozone (O<sub>3</sub>) absorbs harmful solar ultraviolet radiation (&lambda; &lt; 300 nm). Without this protective shield, high-energy UV photons would cause skin cancers, severe cataracts, and cellular DNA damage.</li>\n    <li>• <b>Greenhouse Effect &amp; Infrared Trapping:</b> Earth's surface absorbs short-wavelength visible light from the Sun and re-emits long-wavelength Infrared radiation. Atmospheric greenhouse gases (CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>O vapor) absorb this outgoing IR radiation, trapping heat and maintaining Earth's average surface temperature at a hospitable +15&deg;C (without greenhouse effect, Earth would freeze at -18&deg;C!).</li>\n    <li>• <b>Why Fog Lights and Danger Signals Use Red Light:</b> Rayleigh scattering states that scattering intensity is inversely proportional to the 4th power of wavelength: <b>I<sub>scattered</sub> &prop; 1/&lambda;<sup>4</sup></b>. Red light has the longest visible wavelength (&lambda; &approx; 700 nm), so it scatters the least through fog and dust, traveling longest distances without attenuation.</li>\n  </ul>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-top: 35px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center;\">⚡ Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet</h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin-top: 14px; font-size: 14.5px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Displacement Current:</b> I<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub> / dt) = C (dV / dt)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Ampere-Maxwell Law:</b> &oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (I<sub>c</sub> + I<sub>d</sub>)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>EM Wave Vacuum Speed:</b> c = 1 / &radic;(&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) = 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Medium Speed:</b> v = 1 / &radic;(&mu; &epsilon;) = c / n</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Amplitude Ratio:</b> E<sub>0</sub> / B<sub>0</sub> = E / B = c</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Wave Relations:</b> c = &nu; &lambda; = &omega; / k &nbsp;|&nbsp; k = 2&pi; / &lambda;</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Energy Density:</b> u<sub>avg</sub> = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup></div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Intensity:</b> I = u<sub>avg</sub> c = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> c</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Momentum Delivered:</b> p = U / c (absorption) &nbsp;|&nbsp; p = 2U / c (reflection)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Radiation Pressure:</b> P<sub>rad</sub> = I / c (absorption) &nbsp;|&nbsp; P<sub>rad</sub> = 2I / c (reflection)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Poynting Vector:</b> <b>S</b> = (1/&mu;<sub>0</sub>) (<b>E</b> &times; <b>B</b>)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Rayleigh Scattering:</b> I<sub>scattered</sub> &prop; 1 / &lambda;<sup>4</sup></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";

export const c12Phy5HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #FFFFFF; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 16px;\">\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #00E5FF; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📝 Comprehensive Board Question Bank &amp; Step-by-Step Solutions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 14px; margin: 0; text-align: center !important;\">Unit V: Electromagnetic Waves &bull; Exactly 100 Graduated Questions (Sections A, B, C)</p>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00E5FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00E5FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">SECTION A: VERY SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS (1 MARK EACH &ndash; Q1 TO Q40)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q1: Define displacement current and write its mathematical formula.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Displacement current is the current arising in a region of space due to a time-varying electric field or time-varying electric flux: <b>I<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>E</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q2: State the SI unit and dimensional formula of displacement current.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The SI unit of displacement current is the <b>Ampere (A)</b>. Its dimensional formula is <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup> A<sup>1</sup>]</b> (identical to conduction current).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q3: What missing term did Maxwell add to Ampere's circuital law?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Maxwell added the <b>displacement current term &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>d</sub> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub>/dt)</b> to Ampere's circuital law, creating the Ampere-Maxwell law.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q4: State the Ampere-Maxwell law in mathematical form.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (I<sub>c</sub> + I<sub>d</sub>) = &mu;<sub>0</sub> [ I<sub>c</sub> + &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>E</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> ]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q5: What is the value of displacement current inside a parallel plate capacitor connected across a steady DC battery after it is fully charged?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Zero (I<sub>d</sub> = 0)</b>, because once the capacitor is fully charged by DC, the voltage and electric flux become constant (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub>/dt = 0).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q6: What is the magnitude of displacement current in the connecting wires of a charging capacitor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Zero (I<sub>d</sub> = 0)</b>, because in the conducting wires, the electric field is negligible and total current flows purely as conduction current (I<sub>c</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q7: How is displacement current related to conduction current during the charging of a capacitor?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Displacement current between the plates is <b>strictly equal in magnitude</b> to the conduction current in the connecting wires at every instant: <b>I<sub>d</sub> = I<sub>c</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q8: Write the expression for displacement current in terms of capacitance C and rate of change of voltage dV/dt.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>I<sub>d</sub> = C <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dV</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q9: What is the source of an electromagnetic wave?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      An <b>accelerating or oscillating electric charge</b> (which produces time-varying electric and magnetic fields regenerating each other).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q10: Can a stationary charge produce electromagnetic waves? Why?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>No</b>. A stationary charge produces only a static electrostatic field (<b>E</b>) and zero magnetic field, so no radiation occurs.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q11: Can a charge moving with uniform velocity radiate electromagnetic waves? Why?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>No</b>. A charge moving with uniform velocity produces a steady magnetic field (d<b>B</b>/dt = 0) and static electric current, which cannot regenerate time-varying fields.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q12: Write the expression for the speed of electromagnetic waves in vacuum in terms of fundamental constants.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)</span></span> &approx; 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q13: Write the relation between the speed of an EM wave in vacuum (c) and in a dielectric medium of refractive index n.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>v = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(&mu; &epsilon;)</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(&mu;<sub>r</sub> &epsilon;<sub>r</sub>)</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q14: State the relation connecting the amplitudes of electric and magnetic fields in an EM wave in vacuum.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">E<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>0</sub></span></span> = c</b> (or E = c B at every instant).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q15: Why are electromagnetic waves called transverse waves?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Because both the electric field vector <b>E</b> and magnetic field vector <b>B</b> oscillate in directions <b>perpendicular to each other and perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q16: Write the unit vector representing the direction of propagation of an EM wave.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>k̂ = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\"><b>E</b> &times; <b>B</b></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|<b>E</b> &times; <b>B</b>|</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q17: What is the phase difference between electric and magnetic field oscillations in a vacuum plane EM wave?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Zero (0 radians)</b>. The electric and magnetic fields oscillate in phase, reaching their maximums and zero-crossings at the exact same instant.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q18: Which vector in an electromagnetic wave is responsible for optical sensations and photographic effects?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The <b>Electric field vector <b>E</b></b> (called the <b>optical vector</b>) because electrical forces on electrons are c times stronger than magnetic forces (F<sub>e</sub> &gt;&gt; F<sub>m</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q19: State the total average energy density of an electromagnetic wave in vacuum.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>u<sub>avg</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span></b> [J m<sup>-3</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q20: Write the formula for the intensity I of an electromagnetic wave in terms of peak electric field E<sub>0</sub>.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>I = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> c = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup> c</b> [W m<sup>-2</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q21: Define Poynting vector and state its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Poynting vector <b><b>S</b> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span> (<b>E</b> &times; <b>B</b>)</b> represents the rate of electromagnetic energy transfer per unit cross-sectional area. Its SI unit is <b>Watt per square meter (W/m<sup>2</sup>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q22: Write the expression for linear momentum p transferred by an EM wave of energy U to a completely absorbing surface.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>p = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">U</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q23: Write the expression for radiation pressure P<sub>rad</sub> exerted on a perfectly reflecting surface by an EM wave of intensity I.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>P<sub>rad</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span></b> [N m<sup>-2</sup> or Pa].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q24: Arrange in order of increasing frequency: Microwaves, X-rays, Infrared, Gamma rays.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Microwaves &lt; Infrared &lt; X-rays &lt; Gamma rays</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q25: Arrange in order of increasing wavelength: Ultraviolet, Visible light, Radio waves, X-rays.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>X-rays &lt; Ultraviolet &lt; Visible light &lt; Radio waves</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q26: Which part of the electromagnetic spectrum has the highest penetrating power?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Gamma (&gamma;) rays</b>, due to their extremely high frequency (&nu; &gt; 3 &times; 10<sup>19</sup> Hz) and highest photon energy (E = h&nu;).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q27: Which electromagnetic waves are produced by rapid decelerations of high-speed electrons striking a heavy metal target?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>X-Rays (Roentgen rays)</b> (in a Coolidge X-ray tube).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q28: Which electromagnetic waves are produced by radioactive transitions in atomic nuclei?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Gamma (&gamma;) rays</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q29: Which EM waves are used in RADAR systems for aircraft navigation and why?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Microwaves</b>, because their short wavelengths (1 mm to 0.1 m) allow them to be beamed in narrow, directional beams with minimal diffraction.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q30: Which electromagnetic waves are used in water purifiers to kill bacteria and germs?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Ultraviolet (UV-C) radiation</b> (&lambda; &approx; 254 nm).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q31: Why are infrared waves called heat waves?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Because infrared frequencies match the vibrational frequencies of molecules in most materials, causing them to absorb IR radiation and convert it directly into internal thermal kinetic energy (heat).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q32: Which layer of the Earth's atmosphere absorbs harmful solar ultraviolet radiation?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The <b>Stratospheric Ozone Layer (O<sub>3</sub>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q33: Name the EM waves used in television and cellular phone communications.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Radio waves</b> (VHF/UHF bands) and <b>Microwaves</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q34: What is the wavelength range of visible light in Angstroms (&Aring;)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>4000 &Aring; (Violet, 400 nm) to 7000 &Aring; (Red, 700 nm)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q35: Which physical property is identical for all electromagnetic waves propagating in vacuum?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Their <b>speed of propagation (c = 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q36: Name the device used to produce microwaves.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Magnetron valve</b>, <b>Klystron tube</b>, or <b>Gunn diode</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q37: Why is the human eye sensitive to only a narrow band of the EM spectrum (visible light)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The maximum intensity of solar radiation reaching the Earth's surface peaks in the visible region (400–700 nm), and biological photoreceptor pigments (rhodopsin in retinal cones/rods) evolved to absorb these specific photon energies (1.8 eV to 3.1 eV).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q38: Why do fog lights on automobiles use yellow/red light instead of blue light?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      According to Rayleigh's scattering law (I &prop; 1/&lambda;<sup>4</sup>), longer wavelengths (yellow/red) scatter far less through fog droplets, penetrating longer distances without blinding glare.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q39: What is the frequency of an electromagnetic wave having a vacuum wavelength of 30 cm?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      &nu; = c / &lambda; = (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s) / (0.30 m) = <b>10<sup>9</sup> Hz = 1 GHz (Microwave)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q40: State the condition under which an LC resonant circuit radiates electromagnetic waves effectively.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      When the physical dimensions of the circuit/antenna are comparable to the wavelength of radiation (length l &approx; &lambda;/4 or &lambda;/2) and oscillating at high frequencies.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00E5FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00E5FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">SECTION B: SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS (2 &amp; 3 MARKS EACH &ndash; Q41 TO Q80)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q41: A parallel plate capacitor with circular plates of radius 12 cm is being charged by an external current of 0.15 A. Calculate the displacement current across the plates.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Concept:</b> By Maxwell's continuity of total current, the displacement current I<sub>d</sub> across the vacuum gap between capacitor plates is strictly equal to the conduction current I<sub>c</sub> supplying the plates.<br>2. <b>Calculation:</b> I<sub>d</sub> = I<sub>c</sub> = <b>0.15 Amperes</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q42: A parallel plate capacitor of capacitance C = 2 &mu;F is connected across an AC voltage source V = 200 sin(100&pi; t). Find the maximum displacement current between the plates.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Formula:</b> I<sub>d</sub>(t) = C (dV/dt) = C <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> [V<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t)] = C V<sub>0</sub> &omega; cos(&omega;t).<br>2. <b>Peak Displacement Current:</b> I<sub>d,max</sub> = C V<sub>0</sub> &omega;.<br>3. <b>Calculation:</b> Given C = 2 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> F, V<sub>0</sub> = 200 V, &omega; = 100&pi; rad/s.<br>I<sub>d,max</sub> = (2 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>) &times; 200 &times; (100&pi;) = 4 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> &times; 314.16 = <b>0.126 A (125.7 mA)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q43: In a plane EM wave, the peak electric field is E<sub>0</sub> = 120 N/C and frequency is 50 MHz. Determine (a) B<sub>0</sub>, (b) &omega;, (c) k, (d) &lambda;.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: E<sub>0</sub> = 120 N/C, &nu; = 50 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> Hz, c = 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s.<br>(a) Peak Magnetic Field: B<sub>0</sub> = E<sub>0</sub> / c = 120 / (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) = <b>4.0 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> Tesla (400 nT)</b>.<br>(b) Angular Frequency: &omega; = 2&pi; &nu; = 2 &times; 3.1416 &times; (50 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) = <b>3.14 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> rad/s</b>.<br>(c) Propagation Constant: k = &omega; / c = (3.14 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) / (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) = <b>1.05 rad/m</b>.<br>(d) Wavelength: &lambda; = c / &nu; = (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) / (50 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) = <b>6.0 meters</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q44: Write the mathematical wave equations for electric and magnetic fields of an EM wave propagating along +z axis with electric field along +x axis.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Electric Field Vector (along +x axis):</b> <b>E</b>(z, t) = E<sub>0</sub> sin(k z - &omega; t) <b>î</b>.<br>2. <b>Magnetic Field Vector (along +y axis):</b> <b>B</b>(z, t) = B<sub>0</sub> sin(k z - &omega; t) <b>ĵ</b>.<br>3. <b>Check:</b> Direction of propagation is <b>î</b> &times; <b>ĵ</b> = <b>k̂</b> (+z axis), confirming orthogonality.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q45: Show that the dimensions of the product &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> are equal to [L<sup>-2</sup> T<sup>2</sup>].</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. From Maxwell's wave speed formula: c = 1 / &radic;(&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) &rArr; c<sup>2</sup> = 1 / (&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>) &rArr; &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> = 1 / c<sup>2</sup>.<br>2. Dimensions of speed c: [c] = [L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>].<br>3. Dimensions of 1/c<sup>2</sup>: [1/c<sup>2</sup>] = 1 / [L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]<sup>2</sup> = <b>[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>-2</sup> T<sup>2</sup>]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q46: Show that the average energy density of an electric field in an EM wave equals the average energy density of the magnetic field.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Electric energy density: u<sub>E</sub> = 1/4 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>.<br>2. Substitute E<sub>0</sub> = c B<sub>0</sub> and c<sup>2</sup> = 1 / (&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>):<br>u<sub>E</sub> = 1/4 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (c B<sub>0</sub>)<sup>2</sup> = 1/4 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> [1 / (&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span> = u<sub>B</sub></b>.<br>Hence, energy is shared equally between electric and magnetic fields (50% each).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q47: A plane electromagnetic wave of intensity I = 1000 W/m<sup>2</sup> is incident normally on a completely absorbing surface of area 2.0 m<sup>2</sup>. Calculate the force exerted on the surface.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Radiation Pressure (for absorption):</b> P<sub>rad</sub> = I / c = 1000 / (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) = 3.33 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> N/m<sup>2</sup>.<br>2. <b>Force Exerted:</b> F = P<sub>rad</sub> &times; Area = (3.33 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>) &times; 2.0 = <b>6.67 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> Newtons</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q48: How would the force in the previous question change if the surface was completely reflecting?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      For a completely reflecting surface, the radiation pressure doubles because momentum reverses on reflection: P<sub>rad</sub> = 2 I / c.<br>Therefore, Force = 2 &times; (6.67 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> N) = <b>1.33 &times; 10<sup>-5</sup> Newtons (doubled)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q49: Explain how Maxwell modified Ampere's circuital law and state the physical reason for this modification.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Flaw:</b> Ampere's original law &oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I gave conflicting results for a charging capacitor when evaluated over a flat surface (intersecting wire, I &ne; 0) versus a bulging surface (passing between plates, I = 0).<br>2. <b>Resolution:</b> Maxwell recognized that the time-varying electric flux between the plates produces an equivalent <b>displacement current I<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub>/dt)</b>.<br>3. <b>Modified Law:</b> &oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (I<sub>c</sub> + I<sub>d</sub>), making the law universally consistent for all surfaces.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q50: Why is the ozone layer in the stratosphere crucial for human survival? What would happen if it were depleted?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Function:</b> The stratospheric ozone layer (O<sub>3</sub>) absorbs high-energy solar ultraviolet radiation (UV-B and UV-C, &lambda; &lt; 300 nm).<br>2. <b>Impact of Depletion:</b> Unfiltered UV radiation reaching the Earth's surface would cause widespread skin cancer (melanoma), severe corneal damage (cataracts), genetic cellular mutations, and destruction of marine phytoplankton that form the base of ocean food chains.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q51: Explain the working principle of a Microwave Oven and why metal containers must not be used in it.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Principle:</b> Microwave ovens operate at a frequency of 2.45 GHz (&lambda; &approx; 12.2 cm), which matches the natural rotational frequency of polar water molecules in food. Resonant absorption causes intense molecular rotational agitation, heating food from within uniformly.<br>2. <b>Danger of Metals:</b> Metals reflect microwaves and develop large circulating surface eddy currents, creating dangerous electrical arcing and sparking that can ignite fire and damage the magnetron.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q52: Distinguish between Conduction Current and Displacement Current (3 points).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Origin:</b> Conduction current is the physical flow of free electrons; displacement current is caused by time-varying electric flux.<br>2. <b>Medium:</b> Conduction current requires a conducting wire; displacement current exists in dielectrics and perfect vacuum.<br>3. <b>Heating:</b> Conduction current produces I<sup>2</sup>R Joule heat; displacement current in vacuum causes zero Joule dissipation.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q53: Calculate the wavelength of an EM wave of frequency 10 GHz in (a) vacuum, (b) glass of refractive index 1.5.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: &nu; = 10 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> Hz.<br>(a) In Vacuum: &lambda;<sub>0</sub> = c / &nu; = (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) / (10<sup>10</sup>) = <b>0.03 m = 3.0 cm (Microwave)</b>.<br>(b) In Glass: v = c / n = (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) / 1.5 = 2 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s &rArr; &lambda;<sub>glass</sub> = v / &nu; = (2 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) / (10<sup>10</sup>) = <b>0.02 m = 2.0 cm</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q54: A parallel plate capacitor is charged to 100 V. The plate area is 100 cm<sup>2</sup> and plate separation is 1 mm. If charging current is 1 mA, find the rate of change of electric field between plates.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Conduction current I<sub>c</sub> = Displacement current I<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A (dE/dt).<br>2. dE/dt = I<sub>c</sub> / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A) = (10<sup>-3</sup> A) / [(8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) &times; (100 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> m<sup>2</sup>)].<br>3. dE/dt = 10<sup>-3</sup> / (8.854 &times; 10<sup>-14</sup>) = <b>1.13 &times; 10<sup>10</sup> V m<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q55: Name the electromagnetic radiation used in: (a) LASIK eye surgery, (b) Remote controls, (c) Luggage inspection at airports, (d) Treatment of muscular strain.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      (a) LASIK Eye Surgery: <b>Ultraviolet (UV) excimer laser</b>.<br>(b) TV Remote Controls: <b>Infrared (IR) rays</b>.<br>(c) Airport Baggage Inspection: <b>X-Rays</b>.<br>(d) Muscular Strain Treatment: <b>Infrared (IR) heat radiation</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q56: Name the electromagnetic radiation used in: (a) Destroying cancerous cells, (b) Detecting forged bank notes, (c) Satellite communication, (d) Greenhouse heating.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      (a) Cancer Radiotherapy: <b>Gamma (&gamma;) rays (Cobalt-60)</b>.<br>(b) Detecting Forged Bank Notes: <b>Ultraviolet (UV) rays</b>.<br>(c) Satellite Communication: <b>Microwaves</b>.<br>(d) Greenhouse Heating: <b>Infrared (IR) radiation</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q57: A beam of light travels along +y direction. Its electric field oscillates along the +z direction. What is the direction of oscillation of its magnetic field?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Direction of wave propagation: <b>k̂ = +ĵ</b>.<br>2. Electric field direction: <b>Ê = +k̂</b>.<br>3. Relationship: <b>k̂ = Ê &times; B̂</b> &rArr; <b>+ĵ = +k̂ &times; B̂</b>.<br>4. Using cross-product rules: <b>k̂ &times; (+î) = +ĵ</b>.<br>Therefore, the magnetic field vector <b>B</b> oscillates along the <b>+x axis (î)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q58: The magnetic field in a plane EM wave is given by B<sub>y</sub> = 2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> sin(0.5 &times; 10<sup>3</sup> x + 1.5 &times; 10<sup>11</sup> t) Tesla. Find the amplitude and expression for the electric field.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Amplitude: E<sub>0</sub> = c B<sub>0</sub> = (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) &times; (2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) = <b>60 V/m</b>.<br>2. Direction: Wave travels along -x direction, <b>B</b> is along y-axis, so <b>E</b> must oscillate along the z-axis.<br>3. Expression: <b>E<sub>z</sub> = 60 sin(0.5 &times; 10<sup>3</sup> x + 1.5 &times; 10<sup>11</sup> t) V/m</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q59: Explain why radio waves diffract easily around buildings and hills, while visible light waves cannot.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Diffraction occurs significantly only when the size of the obstacle is comparable to the wavelength of the wave (d &approx; &lambda;). Radio waves have very large wavelengths (&lambda; &approx; 1 m to 1 km), comparable to the size of buildings and hills, causing noticeable bending. Visible light has microscopic wavelengths (&lambda; &approx; 10<sup>-7</sup> m), so it travels in straight lines with negligible diffraction around macroscopic objects.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q60: Explain how the Greenhouse Effect warms the Earth's surface.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Solar radiation (mostly visible light of short wavelength) easily penetrates the Earth's atmosphere and heats the ground.<br>2. The warm Earth re-radiates energy as long-wavelength <b>Infrared (IR) radiation</b>.<br>3. Atmospheric greenhouse gases (CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, water vapor) absorb this outgoing IR radiation and re-emit it in all directions, trapping heat in the lower atmosphere and keeping Earth's climate hospitable.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q61: State the main difference between X-rays and Gamma rays in terms of: (a) Origin/Production, (b) Frequency.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      (a) <b>Origin:</b> X-rays are produced by transitions of inner-shell atomic electrons or sudden deceleration of fast electrons (extranuclear origin); Gamma rays originate strictly from radioactive nuclear transitions inside the atomic nucleus (nuclear origin).<br>(b) <b>Frequency:</b> Gamma rays generally have higher frequencies (&nu; &gt; 3 &times; 10<sup>19</sup> Hz) and higher photon energies than X-rays (&nu; &approx; 10<sup>16</sup> to 10<sup>19</sup> Hz).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q62: A 100 W incandescent bulb converts 5% of electrical energy into visible light. What is the average intensity of visible radiation at a distance of 2.5 m from the bulb?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Visible Optical Power: P<sub>vis</sub> = 5% of 100 W = 5.0 Watts.<br>2. The light radiates uniformly over a sphere of radius r = 2.5 m: Area A = 4&pi; r<sup>2</sup> = 4 &times; 3.1416 &times; (2.5)<sup>2</sup> = 78.54 m<sup>2</sup>.<br>3. Average Intensity: I = P<sub>vis</sub> / A = 5.0 / 78.54 = <b>0.0637 W/m<sup>2</sup> (63.7 mW/m<sup>2</sup>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q63: For the previous problem, calculate the peak electric field E<sub>0</sub> at distance 2.5 m.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Formula: I = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> c &rArr; E<sub>0</sub> = &radic;[ 2 I / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> c) ].<br>2. Calculation: E<sub>0</sub> = &radic;[ (2 &times; 0.0637) / ((8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) &times; (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>)) ] = &radic;[ 0.1274 / (2.656 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) ] = &radic;47.97 = <b>6.93 V/m</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q64: Why is an electrostatic field conservative while the electric field associated with an electromagnetic wave is non-conservative?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      An electrostatic field is produced by static charges, and its line integral around any closed loop is strictly zero (&oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = 0), meaning work done is path-independent (conservative). In an EM wave, the electric field is induced by a time-varying magnetic field according to Faraday's law (&oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = - d&Phi;<sub>B</sub>/dt &ne; 0), forming closed loop field lines and making it strictly non-conservative.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q65: A laser beam has an intensity of 3.0 &times; 10<sup>14</sup> W/m<sup>2</sup>. Find the peak amplitudes of electric and magnetic fields in the beam.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. E<sub>0</sub> = &radic;[ 2 I / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> c) ] = &radic;[ (2 &times; 3.0 &times; 10<sup>14</sup>) / ((8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) &times; (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>)) ] = &radic;[ 6.0 &times; 10<sup>14</sup> / (2.656 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) ] = &radic;[2.259 &times; 10<sup>17</sup>] = <b>4.75 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> V/m</b>.<br>2. B<sub>0</sub> = E<sub>0</sub> / c = (4.75 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) / (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) = <b>1.58 Tesla</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q66: Why are short waves used in long-distance radio broadcasting?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Short radio waves (High Frequency HF band, 3 MHz to 30 MHz) are reflected back to Earth by the charged <b>Ionosphere layer</b> of the upper atmosphere (Sky Wave Propagation), allowing signals to bounce repeatedly between ionosphere and ground over thousands of kilometers across the globe.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q67: Why do television broadcast signals use UHF/VHF frequencies rather than short waves?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      TV signals carry high-bandwidth video information requiring high carrier frequencies (VHF/UHF, 54 MHz to 890 MHz). These high frequencies penetrate right through the ionosphere without being reflected, so TV broadcasts rely on straight <b>line-of-sight space wave propagation</b> from tall transmission towers.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q68: What was Heinrich Hertz's historic contribution to electromagnetic waves in 1887?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Heinrich Hertz experimentally produced and detected electromagnetic waves for the first time using an oscillating high-voltage spark-gap LC circuit. He proved that these spark-generated waves traveled at the speed of light and underwent reflection, refraction, and polarization, completely validating Maxwell's electromagnetic theory.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q69: What was Jagadish Chandra Bose's contribution to electromagnetic wave research?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      In 1895 in Kolkata, Indian physicist <b>Sir J.C. Bose</b> successfully generated and detected extremely high-frequency millimeter microwaves (&lambda; &approx; 5 mm to 25 mm) using innovative spark generators and semiconductor crystal detectors (coherers).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q70: What was Guglielmo Marconi's breakthrough in electromagnetic waves in 1899?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Guglielmo Marconi attached long vertical antenna wires and earth connections to transmit radio wave signals across several kilometers, and in 1901 achieved the first transatlantic wireless radio transmission across the Atlantic Ocean, founding modern telecommunications.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q71: If the Earth had no atmosphere, what would be its average surface temperature and why?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Without an atmosphere, there would be no greenhouse gases to trap outgoing infrared thermal radiation. All re-radiated heat would escape instantly into outer space, plunging the Earth's average surface temperature from its current hospitable +15&deg;C down to a bitterly frozen <b>-18&deg;C (255 K)</b>, making life impossible.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q72: A capacitor has plates of area A and separation d. A charging current i(t) flows into it. Prove that the displacement current density J<sub>d</sub> equals &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (dE/dt).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Displacement current: I<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub>/dt) = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (d/dt)[E A] = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A (dE/dt).<br>2. Displacement current density J<sub>d</sub> is current per unit plate area:<br>J<sub>d</sub> = I<sub>d</sub> / A = [ &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A (dE/dt) ] / A = <b>&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dE</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q73: Why can humans not see infrared radiation with their eyes, but can feel it on their skin?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      The photon energies of infrared radiation (1.2 meV to 1.7 eV) are too low to trigger chemical isomerization of rhodopsin visual pigments in the human retina. However, IR wavelengths match molecular thermal vibrations, which stimulate cutaneous thermoreceptors in human skin as warmth/heat.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q74: Why are X-ray photographs taken with lead aprons placed over other body parts?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      X-rays consist of highly ionizing photons (E &gt; 100 eV) capable of breaking chemical molecular bonds, ionizing water in cells to produce free radicals, and causing genetic DNA mutations. Lead (Pb) has a very high atomic number (Z = 82) and high density, absorbing X-rays efficiently to protect healthy organs.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q75: State the condition under which a charged particle accelerates in a circular path will emit EM waves.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      A charged particle moving in a circular path (such as in a synchrotron or cyclotron) experiences continuous centripetal acceleration (a = v<sup>2</sup>/r). Because any accelerated charge radiates energy, it continuously emits a broad spectrum of electromagnetic waves (called <b>Synchrotron Radiation</b>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q76: What is meant by the 'skin effect' in high-frequency AC and EM wave propagation in conductors?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      At high frequencies, the changing magnetic flux inside a conductor induces internal eddy currents that oppose current flow in the core and concentrate current density near the outer surface (skin). The depth at which current falls to 1/e is the skin depth &delta; = &radic;[2 / (&omega; &mu; &sigma;)].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q77: A plane EM wave has an electric field amplitude of 3.0 V/m. Calculate (a) the energy density, (b) the magnetic field amplitude.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      (a) Energy density: u<sub>avg</sub> = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = 1/2 &times; (8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) &times; (3.0)<sup>2</sup> = <b>3.98 &times; 10<sup>-11</sup> J/m<sup>3</sup></b>.<br>(b) Magnetic field amplitude: B<sub>0</sub> = E<sub>0</sub> / c = 3.0 / (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) = <b>1.0 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup> Tesla (10 nT)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q78: Why do sunglasses coated with polaroid film reduce glare from wet asphalt and water surfaces better than tinted glass?</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      When sunlight reflects off non-metallic horizontal surfaces (water, wet road) at Brewster's angle, the reflected light becomes horizontally polarized (glare). Polaroid sunglasses have their transmission axis oriented vertically, completely blocking this horizontally polarized reflected glare while allowing natural light through.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q79: Arrange the following in increasing order of penetrating power: Alpha rays, Beta rays, Gamma rays, X-rays.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>Alpha (&alpha;) rays &lt; Beta (&beta;) rays &lt; X-rays &lt; Gamma (&gamma;) rays</b>. (Gamma rays have the shortest wavelength and highest photon energy, penetrating several centimeters of solid lead).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q80: Explain why optical fibers transmit communication signals with virtually zero loss over long distances.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Optical fibers utilize infrared light (&lambda; &approx; 1.3 &mu;m and 1.55 &mu;m) inside ultra-pure silica glass cores where Rayleigh scattering and absorption are minimal. The signal undergoes repetitive <b>Total Internal Reflection (TIR)</b> at the core-cladding boundary, propagating tens of kilometers with minimal attenuation.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00E5FF; border-bottom: 2px solid #00E5FF; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">SECTION C: LONG ANSWER DERIVATIONS &amp; ESSAYS (5 MARKS EACH &ndash; Q81 TO Q100)</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q81: Explain the inconsistency of Ampere's circuital law during the charging of a capacitor. Derive the expression for displacement current and state the generalized Ampere-Maxwell law.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Ampere's Original Circuital Law:</b><br>&oint;<sub>C</sub> <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>enclosed</sub>.<br><b>2. The Contradiction during Capacitor Charging:</b><br>Consider a closed loop C around the wire charging a parallel plate capacitor.<br>• For a flat circular disc surface S<sub>1</sub> spanning loop C: The wire passes through S<sub>1</sub>, so I<sub>enclosed</sub> = I<sub>c</sub> &rArr; &oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>c</sub> &ne; 0.<br>• For a pot-shaped surface S<sub>2</sub> spanning the same loop C whose bottom passes through the gap between capacitor plates: No conduction current crosses S<sub>2</sub>, so I<sub>enclosed</sub> = 0 &rArr; &oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = 0.<br>Because the line integral around loop C cannot have two different values simultaneously, Ampere's law was logically incomplete.<br><b>3. Derivation of Displacement Current:</b><br>As charge q builds on plates of area A, the changing electric field between plates is E = q / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A).<br>Electric flux: &Phi;<sub>E</sub> = E A = q / &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; q = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &Phi;<sub>E</sub>.<br>Differentiating with respect to time:<br>I<sub>c</sub> = dq/dt = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (d&Phi;<sub>E</sub> / dt).<br>Maxwell defined this time-varying flux term as the <b>Displacement Current</b>:<br><span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>I<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>E</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b></span>.<br><b>4. Generalized Ampere-Maxwell Law:</b><br><span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> (I<sub>c</sub> + I<sub>d</sub>) = &mu;<sub>0</sub> [ I<sub>c</sub> + &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>E</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> ]</b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q82: State Maxwell's four equations in integral form and explain the physical significance of each equation in detail.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Gauss's Law in Electrostatics:</b><br>&oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q<sub>in</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&epsilon;<sub>0</sub></span></span>.<br><i>Significance:</i> Relates electric field to source electric charges. Electric field lines originate on positive charges and end on negative charges (isolated monopoles exist).<br><b>2. Gauss's Law in Magnetism:</b><br>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = 0.<br><i>Significance:</i> Total magnetic flux through any closed Gaussian surface is always zero. Magnetic lines of force form continuous closed loops without start or end; <b>isolated magnetic monopoles do not exist</b>.<br><b>3. Faraday-Lenz Law of Induction:</b><br>&oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>B</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> &int; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b>.<br><i>Significance:</i> A time-varying magnetic field induces a non-conservative, closed-loop electric field in space.<br><b>4. Ampere-Maxwell Law:</b><br>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> [ I<sub>c</sub> + &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&Phi;<sub>E</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> ].<br><i>Significance:</i> Magnetic fields are generated by physical conduction currents (moving charges) and by time-varying electric fields (displacement current), establishing total symmetry between electricity and magnetism.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q83: Derive the wave equation for electromagnetic waves in free space from Maxwell's equations, and show that the speed of propagation is c = 1 / &radic;(&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>).</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Free Space Conditions:</b> In vacuum with no free charges (&rho; = 0) and no conduction currents (<b>J</b> = 0):<br>&nabla; &sdot; <b>E</b> = 0, &nbsp; &nabla; &sdot; <b>B</b> = 0, &nbsp; &nabla; &times; <b>E</b> = - &part;<b>B</b>/&part;t, &nbsp; &nabla; &times; <b>B</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &part;<b>E</b>/&part;t.<br><b>2. Taking Curl of Faraday's Law:</b><br>&nabla; &times; (&nabla; &times; <b>E</b>) = &nabla;(&nabla; &sdot; <b>E</b>) - &nabla;<sup>2</sup> <b>E</b> = - &part;/&part;t (&nabla; &times; <b>B</b>).<br>Since &nabla; &sdot; <b>E</b> = 0 and substituting &nabla; &times; <b>B</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &part;<b>E</b>/&part;t:<br>- &nabla;<sup>2</sup> <b>E</b> = - &part;/&part;t [ &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;<b>E</b></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;t</span></span> ] &rArr; <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>&nabla;<sup>2</sup> <b>E</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;<sup>2</sup> <b>E</b></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;t<sup>2</sup></span></span></b></span>.<br><b>3. For 1D Wave Traveling along x-axis:</b><br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;<sup>2</sup> E<sub>y</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;x<sup>2</sup></span></span> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;<sup>2</sup> E<sub>y</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;t<sup>2</sup></span></span>.<br><b>4. Comparison with Standard Wave Equation:</b> Standard wave equation is &part;<sup>2</sup>y/&part;x<sup>2</sup> = (1/v<sup>2</sup>) &part;<sup>2</sup>y/&part;t<sup>2</sup>.<br>Comparing yields: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span></span> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &rArr; <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)</span></span> &approx; 3.0 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s</b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q84: Prove mathematically and conceptually the transverse nature of electromagnetic waves in free space.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Setup:</b> Consider a plane EM wave propagating along the +x axis. The fields depend only on x and t (&part;/&part;y = 0, &part;/&part;z = 0).<br><b>2. Applying Gauss's Law in Free Space (&nabla; &sdot; <b>E</b> = 0):</b><br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;E<sub>x</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;x</span></span> + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;E<sub>y</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;y</span></span> + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;E<sub>z</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;z</span></span> = 0 &rArr; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;E<sub>x</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;x</span></span> = 0.<br>This means E<sub>x</sub> does not vary with space. A static, uniform E<sub>x</sub> cannot form part of a propagating wave, so for the wave field: <b>E<sub>x</sub> = 0</b>.<br><b>3. Applying Gauss's Law in Magnetism (&nabla; &sdot; <b>B</b> = 0):</b><br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;B<sub>x</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;x</span></span> = 0 &rArr; <b>B<sub>x</sub> = 0</b>.<br><b>4. Conclusion:</b> The electric field and magnetic field vectors have zero components along the direction of wave propagation (x-axis). All oscillating field components lie strictly in the transverse y-z plane (<b>E</b> &perp; <b>k̂</b> and <b>B</b> &perp; <b>k̂</b>), rigorously proving that <b>electromagnetic waves are transverse</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q85: Derive the expression for the total energy density of an electromagnetic wave and show that the energy is shared equally between the electric and magnetic fields.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Instantaneous Energy Densities:</b><br>• Electric field: u<sub>E</sub> = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sup>2</sup> = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> [E<sub>0</sub> sin(kx - &omega;t)]<sup>2</sup>.<br>• Magnetic field: u<sub>B</sub> = B<sup>2</sup> / (2&mu;<sub>0</sub>) = [B<sub>0</sub> sin(kx - &omega;t)]<sup>2</sup> / (2&mu;<sub>0</sub>).<br><b>2. Average over One Full Period:</b> Since average value of sin<sup>2</sup>(&theta;) over a cycle is 1/2:<br>&lang;u<sub>E</sub>&rang; = 1/4 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>, &nbsp;&nbsp; &lang;u<sub>B</sub>&rang; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span>.<br><b>3. Equating &lang;u<sub>E</sub>&rang; and &lang;u<sub>B</sub>&rang;:</b><br>Substitute E<sub>0</sub> = c B<sub>0</sub> and c<sup>2</sup> = 1 / (&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>):<br>&lang;u<sub>E</sub>&rang; = 1/4 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (c<sup>2</sup> B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>) = 1/4 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> [1 / (&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>)] B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span> = &lang;u<sub>B</sub>&rang;</b>.<br><b>4. Total Average Energy Density:</b><br><span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>u<sub>avg</sub> = &lang;u<sub>E</sub>&rang; + &lang;u<sub>B</sub>&rang; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span></b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q86: Derive the expression for the intensity of an electromagnetic wave in terms of its electric field amplitude E<sub>0</sub> and speed c.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Definition of Intensity (I):</b> Intensity is defined as the total radiant energy crossing per unit area per unit time perpendicular to wave propagation.<br><b>2. Energy in a Cylinder:</b> Consider an imaginary cylinder of cross-sectional area A and length l = c &Delta;t aligned along the direction of wave travel.<br>• Volume of cylinder: V = A &times; l = A c &Delta;t.<br>• Total energy inside cylinder: U = u<sub>avg</sub> &times; V = u<sub>avg</sub> (A c &Delta;t).<br><b>3. Intensity Formula:</b><br>I = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">U</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A &Delta;t</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sub>avg</sub> (A c &Delta;t)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A &Delta;t</span></span> = <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b>u<sub>avg</sub> &times; c</b></span>.<br><b>4. Substituting u<sub>avg</sub> = 1/2 &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>:</b><br><span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>I = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> c = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> E<sub>rms</sub><sup>2</sup> c</b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q87: Explain the concepts of Linear Momentum, Radiation Pressure, and Poynting Vector for electromagnetic radiation with complete mathematical expressions.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Poynting Vector (<b>S</b>):</b><br>Represents the directional power flux density (rate of energy transfer per unit area):<br><span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b><b>S</b> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span></span> (<b>E</b> &times; <b>B</b>)</b></span> [W/m<sup>2</sup>]. Its time-average magnitude equals wave intensity: &lang;|<b>S</b>|&rang; = I.<br><b>2. Linear Momentum of Radiation:</b><br>When electromagnetic energy U is absorbed by a surface, the momentum transferred is:<br>• Total Absorption: <b>p = U / c</b>.<br>• Total Reflection: <b>p = 2 U / c</b> (momentum changes from +U/c to -U/c, giving &Delta;p = 2U/c).<br><b>3. Radiation Pressure (P<sub>rad</sub>):</b><br>Force per unit area exerted by radiation:<br>• For Absorbing Surface: <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>P<sub>rad</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">F</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dp/dt</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(1/c) dU/dt</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span></b></span>.<br>• For Reflecting Surface: <span style=\"color: #00E5FF;\"><b>P<sub>rad</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">c</span></span></b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q88: Give a comprehensive classification of the 7 regions of the Electromagnetic Spectrum, specifying their frequency ranges, wavelength bounds, production mechanisms, and two primary applications for each.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Radio Waves (&lambda; &gt; 0.1 m, &nu; &lt; 3 GHz):</b> Produced by oscillating LC circuits. <i>Uses:</i> AM/FM radio broadcasting, cellular communications.<br><b>2. Microwaves (0.1 m &gt; &lambda; &gt; 1 mm, 1 GHz to 300 GHz):</b> Produced by Klystrons/Magnetrons. <i>Uses:</i> RADAR aircraft navigation, Microwave ovens.<br><b>3. Infrared Waves (1 mm &gt; &lambda; &gt; 700 nm, 3&times;10<sup>11</sup> to 4&times;10<sup>14</sup> Hz):</b> Produced by hot bodies and molecular vibrations. <i>Uses:</i> TV remotes, Night-vision cameras, physical therapy.<br><b>4. Visible Light (700 nm &gt; &lambda; &gt; 400 nm, 4&times;10<sup>14</sup> to 7.5&times;10<sup>14</sup> Hz):</b> Produced by atomic electron de-excitations. <i>Uses:</i> Human vision, optical photography, photosynthesis.<br><b>5. Ultraviolet (400 nm &gt; &lambda; &gt; 1 nm, 7.5&times;10<sup>14</sup> to 10<sup>16</sup> Hz):</b> Produced by high-voltage arcs and solar radiation. <i>Uses:</i> Water sterilization, LASIK surgery, counterfeit detection.<br><b>6. X-Rays (1 nm &gt; &lambda; &gt; 10<sup>-3</sup> nm, 10<sup>16</sup> to 3&times;10<sup>19</sup> Hz):</b> Produced by fast electrons hitting metal targets. <i>Uses:</i> Medical bone radiography, airport security scanners, crystal structure analysis.<br><b>7. Gamma Rays (&lambda; &lt; 10<sup>-3</sup> nm, &nu; &gt; 3&times;10<sup>19</sup> Hz):</b> Produced by radioactive nuclear decays. <i>Uses:</i> Cancer tumor radiotherapy, medical instrument sterilization.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q89: Describe Heinrich Hertz's historic experimental setup for the production and detection of electromagnetic waves.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b>1. Objective:</b> To verify Maxwell's theoretical prediction that oscillating charges radiate electromagnetic waves traveling at speed c.<br><b>2. Transmitter Setup:</b> Consisted of two large metallic spheres connected to two small spark-gap balls across the secondary of an induction coil. When high voltage broke down the spark gap, high-frequency oscillatory electric currents flowed across the gap.<br><b>3. Detection Setup:</b> Placed several meters away, consisting of an open circular wire ring with tiny polished spark spheres. When the transmitter spark fired, magnetic and electric waves traveled through air and induced a spark in the detector ring gap.<br><b>4. Verification:</b> Hertz demonstrated that these waves traveled at 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s, underwent reflection from zinc plates, refracted through asphalt prisms, and were polarized, conclusively confirming electromagnetic wave theory.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q90: A parallel plate capacitor consists of two circular plates of radius R = 6.0 cm separated by distance d = 2.0 mm. It is charged at a steady rate of 0.20 A. (a) Calculate the rate of change of electric field. (b) Find the displacement current across the plates. (c) Calculate the magnetic field B at distance r = 3.0 cm from the central axis. (d) Calculate B at distance r = 12 cm.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: R = 0.06 m, A = &pi; R<sup>2</sup> = 3.1416 &times; (0.06)<sup>2</sup> = 1.131 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup> m<sup>2</sup>, I<sub>c</sub> = 0.20 A.<br>(a) dE/dt = I<sub>c</sub> / (&epsilon;<sub>0</sub> A) = 0.20 / [(8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) &times; (1.131 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup>)] = <b>2.0 &times; 10<sup>12</sup> V m<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br>(b) Displacement Current: I<sub>d</sub> = I<sub>c</sub> = <b>0.20 A</b>.<br>(c) Inside (r &le; R): &oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>d,enc</sub> &rArr; B (2&pi; r) = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>d</sub> (r<sup>2</sup> / R<sup>2</sup>) &rArr; B = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>d</sub> r) / (2&pi; R<sup>2</sup>).<br>For r = 0.03 m: B = [(4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; 0.20 &times; 0.03] / [2&pi; &times; (0.06)<sup>2</sup>] = <b>3.33 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> Tesla (333 nT)</b>.<br>(d) Outside (r &gt; R): B (2&pi; r) = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>d</sub> &rArr; B = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>d</sub>) / (2&pi; r).<br>For r = 0.12 m: B = [(4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; 0.20] / [2&pi; &times; 0.12] = <b>3.33 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> Tesla (333 nT)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q91: Explain why a changing electric field produces a magnetic field and vice versa, and how this mutual regeneration gives birth to an electromagnetic wave.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Faraday's Principle:</b> When a magnetic field varies with time (&part;<b>B</b>/&part;t &ne; 0), it creates an electric field in space (&nabla; &times; <b>E</b> = - &part;<b>B</b>/&part;t).<br>2. <b>Maxwell's Principle:</b> Conversely, when an electric field varies with time (&part;<b>E</b>/&part;t &ne; 0), it generates a magnetic field in space (&nabla; &times; <b>B</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> &part;<b>E</b>/&part;t).<br>3. <b>Mutual Regeneration:</b> An accelerating charge creates a time-varying electric field &rarr; which generates a time-varying magnetic field &rarr; which induces another changing electric field further ahead in space.<br>4. <b>Wave Propagation:</b> Neither field can exist statically in isolation; together, they propagate as a self-sustaining coupled wave carrying energy and momentum through empty space at the universal speed c = 1/&radic;(&mu;<sub>0</sub> &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q92: A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 25 MHz travels in free space along the +x direction. At a particular point in space and time, E = 6.3 V/m along the +y direction. (a) What is B at this point? (b) Write expressions for E and B as functions of x and t.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: &nu; = 25 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> Hz, E = 6.3 V/m along +y, wave along +x.<br>(a) Magnetic field magnitude: B = E / c = 6.3 / (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) = <b>2.1 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup> Tesla (21 nT)</b> along +z axis.<br>(b) Angular frequency: &omega; = 2&pi;&nu; = 2 &times; 3.1416 &times; (25 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) = 1.57 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> rad/s.<br>Wavenumber: k = &omega; / c = (1.57 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) / (3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup>) = 0.524 rad/m.<br><b>E</b>(x, t) = 6.3 sin(0.524 x - 1.57 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> t) <b>ĵ</b> V/m.<br><b>B</b>(x, t) = 2.1 &times; 10<sup>-8</sup> sin(0.524 x - 1.57 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> t) <b>k̂</b> Tesla.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q93: Discuss the environmental impact of Greenhouse Gases and how human industrial activities amplify the natural greenhouse effect.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Natural Greenhouse Effect:</b> Gases such as water vapor, CO<sub>2</sub>, and methane naturally trap re-radiated terrestrial infrared radiation, keeping Earth's average temperature at a habitable +15&deg;C.<br>2. <b>Anthropogenic Amplification:</b> Burning fossil fuels, deforestation, and industrial emissions have drastically increased atmospheric CO<sub>2</sub> concentration from 280 ppm to over 420 ppm.<br>3. <b>Consequences of Global Warming:</b> Increased infrared trapping causes global temperature rise, melting polar ice caps, sea-level rise, acidification of oceans, and extreme climate events (hurricanes, droughts, and heatwaves).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q94: Derive the relation E<sub>0</sub> / B<sub>0</sub> = c starting from Faraday's law of electromagnetic induction for a plane sinusoidal wave.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. Let a plane EM wave travel along +x axis: E<sub>y</sub> = E<sub>0</sub> sin(kx - &omega;t), B<sub>z</sub> = B<sub>0</sub> sin(kx - &omega;t).<br>2. Consider an infinitesimal rectangular loop in the x-y plane of length &Delta;x and height h.<br>3. Applying Faraday's law &oint; <b>E</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = - d&Phi;<sub>B</sub>/dt yields the differential form:<br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;E<sub>y</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;x</span></span> = - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;B<sub>z</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&part;t</span></span>.<br>4. Differentiating the field equations:<br>&part;E<sub>y</sub>/&part;x = k E<sub>0</sub> cos(kx - &omega;t), &nbsp;&nbsp; &part;B<sub>z</sub>/&part;t = - &omega; B<sub>0</sub> cos(kx - &omega;t).<br>5. Substituting into the equation:<br>k E<sub>0</sub> cos(kx - &omega;t) = - [- &omega; B<sub>0</sub> cos(kx - &omega;t)] = &omega; B<sub>0</sub> cos(kx - &omega;t).<br>6. Cancelling cosine terms: k E<sub>0</sub> = &omega; B<sub>0</sub> &rArr; <span style=\"color: #76FF03;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">E<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>0</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&omega;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">k</span></span> = c</b></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q95: Explain why electromagnetic waves carry momentum and exert pressure, and describe the concept of Solar Sails for interplanetary spacecraft propulsion.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Origin of Momentum:</b> When an EM wave impinges on charged particles, the electric field <b>E</b> drives them into motion with velocity <b>v</b>. The magnetic field <b>B</b> then exerts a forward magnetic Lorentz force <b>F</b> = q(<b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>) in the direction of wave propagation, transferring momentum p = U/c.<br>2. <b>Solar Sails Concept:</b> A Solar Sail is a gigantic, ultra-thin reflecting membrane deployed by a spacecraft in space. Continuous reflection of sunlight photons transfers momentum (p = 2U/c), creating a steady thrust without carrying onboard chemical rocket fuel, enabling unlimited long-duration interplanetary travel.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q96: Describe the production, detection, and primary safety hazards associated with Ultraviolet (UV) radiation.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Production:</b> Thermal emission from extremely hot bodies (Sun, carbon arc) and gas-discharge lamps (mercury vapor lamps).<br>2. <b>Detection:</b> Photographic plates, photocells, and fluorescence on zinc sulfide screens.<br>3. <b>Classification:</b> UV-A (320–400 nm), UV-B (280–320 nm), UV-C (100–280 nm).<br>4. <b>Hazards:</b> UV-B and UV-C photons have high photon energy sufficient to break DNA chemical bonds, causing severe sunburns, skin carcinomas, premature skin aging, and corneal inflammation (photokeratitis). Welder's goggles and high-SPF sunscreens are used for protection.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q97: Explain the generation of X-Rays in a Coolidge tube, distinguishing between Continuous X-rays (Bremsstrahlung) and Characteristic X-rays.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Coolidge Tube Mechanism:</b> Electrons boiled off a heated tungsten filament are accelerated by a high voltage (20–100 kV) and collide into a heavy metal target (Tungsten/Molybdenum).<br>2. <b>Continuous X-Rays (Bremsstrahlung / Braking Radiation):</b> As fast electrons decelerate upon deflecting near target atomic nuclei, their lost kinetic energy is emitted directly as X-ray photons with a cutoff wavelength &lambda;<sub>min</sub> = hc / (e V).<br>3. <b>Characteristic X-Rays:</b> Incoming high-energy electrons knock out inner-shell electrons (K-shell). Outer electrons (L or M shell) drop down to fill the vacancy, emitting sharp, discrete X-ray spectral peaks characteristic of the target element.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q98: Compare the properties of Light Waves (EM waves) and Sound Waves (Mechanical waves) across 5 distinct physical parameters.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Nature:</b> Light is an Electromagnetic Transverse wave; Sound is a Mechanical Longitudinal pressure wave.<br>2. <b>Medium Requirement:</b> Light travels freely through vacuum; Sound strictly requires a material medium (solid, liquid, gas) and cannot travel in vacuum.<br>3. <b>Speed in Air/Vacuum:</b> Light travels at c &approx; 3 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> m/s; Sound travels at only &approx; 340 m/s in air at 20&deg;C.<br>4. <b>Polarization:</b> Light can be polarized (proving transverse nature); Sound cannot be polarized.<br>5. <b>Speed Variation in Denser Media:</b> Light slows down in denser optical media (v = c/n); Sound travels faster in denser media (v<sub>solid</sub> &gt; v<sub>liquid</sub> &gt; v<sub>gas</sub>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q99: A parallel plate capacitor is made of circular plates of radius R = 10 cm. The capacitor is being charged such that the electric field between plates increases at a rate of 10<sup>12</sup> V m<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>. (a) Calculate displacement current density. (b) Calculate displacement current between plates. (c) Plot variation of induced magnetic field B versus distance r from the axis.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      Given: R = 0.1 m, dE/dt = 10<sup>12</sup> V m<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>.<br>(a) Displacement current density: J<sub>d</sub> = &epsilon;<sub>0</sub> (dE/dt) = (8.854 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) &times; 10<sup>12</sup> = <b>8.854 A/m<sup>2</sup></b>.<br>(b) Displacement Current: I<sub>d</sub> = J<sub>d</sub> &times; (&pi; R<sup>2</sup>) = 8.854 &times; [3.1416 &times; (0.1)<sup>2</sup>] = 8.854 &times; 0.031416 = <b>0.278 Amperes</b>.<br>(c) Magnetic Field Distribution:<br>• For r &le; R (inside): B(r) = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> J<sub>d</sub> / 2) r &prop; r (linearly increases from 0 at center to peak at r = R).<br>• For r &gt; R (outside): B(r) = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>d</sub>) / (2&pi; r) &prop; 1/r (inversely decreases as 1/r).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 198, 255, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00E5FF; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">Q100: Summarize the overall conceptual unification achieved by Maxwell's Electromagnetic Theory and its impact on modern technological civilization.</h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      1. <b>Grand Unification:</b> Maxwell showed that electricity, magnetism, and optics are not isolated disciplines but manifestations of a single unified entity: the <b>Electromagnetic Field</b>.<br>2. <b>Theoretical Triumph:</b> By identifying displacement current, Maxwell completed the fundamental laws of electrodynamics and predicted the existence of electromagnetic waves propagating at the exact speed of light.<br>3. <b>Technological Transformation:</b> Maxwell's equations laid the bedrock foundation for all modern electrical and electronic technologies: wireless radio, television, mobile networks, radar, satellite communications, Wi-Fi, optical fiber internet, medical imaging (MRI, X-rays), and solar power. Without Maxwell's electromagnetic theory, modern digital civilization could not exist!\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";

